---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { marked } from "marked";
import markdown from "../../../content/harper-lee.md?raw";

const html = marked.parse(markdown) as string;
const headings = [...markdown.matchAll(/^#{2,3}\\s+(.+)$/gm)].map((match, index) => {
  const text = match[1].trim();
  const id = text.toLowerCase().replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, "-").replace(/^-|-$/g, "") + "-" + (index + 1);
  return { text, id, level: match[0].startsWith("###") ? 3 : 2 };
});
let headingIndex = 0;
const rendered = html.replace(/<h([23])>/g, (_match, level) => {
  const heading = headings[headingIndex++];
  return heading ? '<h' + level + ' id="' + heading.id + '">' : "<h" + level + ">";
});
---
<BaseLayout title="Meet Harper Lee" description="Explore Harper Lee's life, To Kill a Mockingbird, its characters, historical context, and practical American English.">
  <article class="section lesson-page" id="top">
    <div class="container">
      <a class="back-link" href="/american-english-method/learn/culture-arts/">← American Culture & Arts</a>
      <div class="lesson-meta">
        <span class="eyebrow">AMERICAN WRITERS</span>
        <span class="source-path">HARPER LEE · 1926–2016</span>
      </div>
      <figure class="harper-hero">
        <img src="https://cdn.loc.gov/service/pnp/highsm/08100/08128v.jpg" alt="The Old Courthouse Museum in Monroeville, Alabama, a historic place associated with Harper Lee and To Kill a Mockingbird" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" />
        <figcaption>
          Old Courthouse Museum, Monroeville, Alabama. Photograph by Carol M. Highsmith, May 12, 2010.
          Library of Congress, Prints and Photographs Division.
          <a href="https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2010639942/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">View the original archive record</a>.
          Rights Advisory: No known restrictions on publication.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <section class="harper-gallery" aria-labelledby="harper-gallery-title">
        <h2 id="harper-gallery-title">Monroeville: The Real Place Behind Maycomb</h2>
        <p class="gallery-intro">
          Harper Lee's fictional Maycomb was shaped by memories of Monroeville. These Library of Congress photographs
          document the real courthouse and its literary legacy; they are not film stills or illustrations of fictional scenes.
        </p>
        <div class="harper-gallery-grid">
          <figure>
            <img src="https://cdn.loc.gov/service/pnp/highsm/08100/08105v.jpg" alt="Memorial to Atticus Finch at the Old Courthouse Museum in Monroeville, Alabama" loading="lazy" />
            <figcaption>
              <strong>Atticus Finch memorial</strong>
              A memorial to the fictional lawyer at the Old Courthouse Museum. Photograph by Carol M. Highsmith,
              May 1, 2010.
              <a href="https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2010639919/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Library of Congress record</a>.
              Rights Advisory: No known restrictions on publication.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure>
            <img src="https://cdn.loc.gov/service/pnp/highsm/08100/08133v.jpg" alt="Historic Old Courthouse Museum in Monroeville, Alabama" loading="lazy" />
            <figcaption>
              <strong>The Old Courthouse Museum</strong>
              Another archival view of the courthouse associated with Harper Lee and Truman Capote.
              Photograph by Carol M. Highsmith, April 29, 2010.
              <a href="https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2010639947/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Library of Congress record</a>.
              Rights Advisory: No known restrictions on publication.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <p class="gallery-note">
          The Library of Congress records list no known restrictions on publication for these photographs.
          Each archive record is linked for attribution and rights information.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section class="harper-literary-visuals" aria-labelledby="harper-literary-title">
        <h2 id="harper-literary-title">From the Books to the Screen</h2>
        <p class="gallery-intro">
          These visual references connect the novels with the symbol at the heart of <em>To Kill a Mockingbird</em>
          and its best-known screen adaptation. The bird photograph is a wildlife reference, not an illustration from the novel.
        </p>
        <div class="literary-visual-grid">
          <figure class="mockingbird-figure">
            <img
              src="https://www.fws.gov/sites/default/files/styles/max_650x650/public/2021-11/2020-06-04_AW%20Northern%20mockingbird.JPG?itok=bl0iwvHB"
              alt="Northern mockingbird perched among green branches"
              loading="lazy"
            />
            <figcaption>
              <strong>The mockingbird</strong>
              A real Northern Mockingbird (<em>Mimus polyglottos</em>), the bird whose name gives the novel its title.
              Photograph by Anna Weyers Blades, U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service, November 19, 2021.
              Public domain.
              <a href="https://www.fws.gov/media/northern-mockingbird" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Official image source and usage rights</a>.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
          <div class="literary-links">
            <h3>Book and film references</h3>
            <p>
              <strong><em>To Kill a Mockingbird</em> — first-edition jacket, 1960.</strong>
              The jacket design is credited to Shirley Smith. The Commons file is marked public domain in the United States,
              but its page notes that protection may differ in other countries. We link to the archival reference rather than
              reproduce it on this international learning site.
              <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:To_Kill_a_Mockingbird_(first_edition_cover).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">View the first-edition cover and rights notes</a>.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong><em>Go Set a Watchman</em> — first-edition cover, 2015.</strong>
              The publisher's cover artwork is copyrighted, so it is presented as an external reference, not embedded here.
              <a href="https://www.cbsnews.com/news/cover-design-unveiled-for-new-harper-lee-novel-go-set-a-watchman/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">See the cover announcement and artwork</a>.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong><em>To Kill a Mockingbird</em> — 1962 film poster.</strong>
              The poster is a separate work from the novel and has its own rights history. The Commons record explains its
              United States public-domain status and possible limits elsewhere.
              <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:To_Kill_a_Mockingbird_(1963_US_theatrical_poster).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">View the film poster and rights information</a>.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
      <nav class="lesson-toc" aria-label="Page contents">
        <details>
          <summary>In this feature</summary>
          <ol>
            {headings.map((heading) => (
              <li class:list={{ "toc-subsection": heading.level === 3 }}>
                <a href={"#" + heading.id}>{heading.text}</a>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ol>
        </details>
      </nav>
      <div class="markdown-content" set:html={rendered} />
      <div class="lesson-end"><a href="#top">Back to top ↑</a><a href="/american-english-method/learn/culture-arts/">← American Culture & Arts</a></div>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
<style>
.harper-hero{max-width:900px;margin:2rem auto 2.5rem}
.harper-hero img{display:block;width:100%;max-height:520px;object-fit:cover;object-position:center;border-radius:8px}
.harper-hero figcaption{margin:.65rem auto 0;max-width:760px;color:#6b7280;font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.55;text-align:center}
.harper-hero figcaption a,.harper-gallery figcaption a,.harper-literary-visuals a{color:#8b6b32;text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:3px}
.harper-gallery,.harper-literary-visuals{max-width:1050px;margin:3.5rem auto}
.harper-gallery h2,.harper-literary-visuals h2{text-align:center;margin-bottom:.6rem}
.gallery-intro,.gallery-note{max-width:780px;margin:0 auto 1.5rem;text-align:center;color:#6b7280;line-height:1.65}
.harper-gallery-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr));gap:1.5rem;align-items:start}
.harper-gallery-grid figure{margin:0;min-width:0}
.harper-gallery-grid img{display:block;width:100%;height:300px;object-fit:cover;background:#f4f1e9;border-radius:7px}
.harper-gallery-grid figcaption,.literary-visual-grid figcaption{padding:.75rem .15rem 0;color:#6b7280;font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.6}
.harper-gallery-grid figcaption strong,.literary-visual-grid figcaption strong{display:block;color:var(--color-text, #273244);margin-bottom:.35rem}
.harper-gallery-grid figcaption a,.literary-visual-grid figcaption a{display:inline-block;margin-top:.35rem}
.gallery-note{font-size:.8rem;margin-top:1.25rem}
.literary-visual-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr) minmax(0,1.15fr);gap:2rem;align-items:start}
.mockingbird-figure{margin:0}
.mockingbird-figure img{display:block;width:100%;max-height:380px;object-fit:contain;object-position:center;background:#f4f1e9;border-radius:7px}
.literary-links{display:grid;gap:1rem}
.literary-links h3{margin:0 0 .25rem}
.literary-links p{margin:0;color:var(--color-text, #273244);line-height:1.65}
.literary-links a{display:inline-block;margin-top:.25rem}
@media(max-width:700px){.harper-gallery-grid,.literary-visual-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:1.8rem}.harper-gallery-grid img{height:auto;max-height:360px}.harper-gallery-grid figure,.mockingbird-figure{max-width:560px;margin:0 auto;width:100%}.mockingbird-figure img{max-height:340px}}
:global(.markdown-content .harper-portrait){float:left;width:min(34%,280px);margin:.35rem 1.6rem 1rem 0}
:global(.markdown-content .harper-portrait img){display:block;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:7px}
:global(.markdown-content .harper-portrait figcaption){margin-top:.55rem;color:#6b7280;font-size:.78rem;line-height:1.5}
:global(.markdown-content .harper-portrait figcaption a){color:#8b6b32;text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:3px}
:global(.markdown-content h2){clear:both}
@media(max-width:700px){:global(.markdown-content .harper-portrait){float:none;width:min(100%,360px);margin:1rem auto 1.4rem}}
</style>
